{
 "id": "2026-11-19-c",
 "title": "What if Jupiter became a star? Add 80 Jupiters ⭐",
 "description": "Jupiter would need about 80 times its mass to fuse hydrogen and become a (tiny, red) star. The weird part: adding mass barely makes it bigger, because gravity squeezes it; past a point it even shrinks. The smallest known star, OGLE-TR-122b, is 96 times Jupiter's mass but only 16% wider. What our sky would look like is speculation, tagged as such.\n\nSources: BBC Science Focus (could Jupiter become a star?); ESO press release eso0507 (OGLE-TR-122b); Ethan Siegel, Starts With A Bang (largest possible planet).",
